WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.897
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Thirty-Seven.

2
00:00:03.897 --> 00:00:08.469
Triangles on the same base and in the same parallels are equal in area.

3
00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:15.489
Let triangles A B C and D B C stand on the same base B C and lie between the same parallel lines A D and B C.

4
00:00:15.489 --> 00:00:19.610
We prove that triangle A B C is equal in area to triangle D B C.

5
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:23.859
Extend segment A D in both directions to points E and F.

6
00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:33.840
By Proposition Thirty-One, through point B draw segment B E parallel to segment C A, and through point C draw segment C F parallel to segment B D.

7
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:42.828
Then each of the figures E B C A and D B C F is a parallelogram on the same base B C and between the same parallels B C and E F.

8
00:00:42.828 --> 00:00:48.986
By Proposition Thirty-Five, parallelogram E B C A is equal in area to parallelogram D B C F.

9
00:00:48.986 --> 00:00:57.487
By Proposition Thirty-Four, the diagonal A B bisects parallelogram E B C A, so triangle A B C is half of parallelogram E B C A.

10
00:00:57.487 --> 00:01:04.783
Likewise, the diagonal D C bisects parallelogram D B C F, so triangle D B C is half of parallelogram D B C F.

11
00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:13.820
Since the halves of equal things are equal to one another, triangle A B C is equal in area to triangle D B C, as was required to prove.
